registerCourse({
  code: "CCAR-F",
  name: "Claude Certified Architect",
  tier: "Foundations",
  blurb: "Scenario-based architecture judgement: agentic loops and orchestration, tool and MCP design, Claude Code configuration, prompting, and context reliability.",
  audience: "Solution architects with hands-on experience designing systems on Claude and the Anthropic API.",
  items: 60,
  minutes: 120,
  passScore: 720,
  fee: "$125 USD",
  domains: [
    { id: "R1", name: "Agentic Architecture", weight: 27.0 },
    { id: "R3", name: "Claude Code & Workflows", weight: 20.0 },
    { id: "R4", name: "Prompt Engineering & Output", weight: 20.0 },
    { id: "R2", name: "Tool Design & MCP", weight: 18.0 },
    { id: "R5", name: "Context & Reliability", weight: 15.0 },
  ],
  scenarioDraw: { scenarios: 4, perScenario: 15 },
  scenarios: [
    { id: "support", title: "Customer Support Resolution Agent", text: "You are building a customer support resolution agent with the Claude Agent SDK. It handles high-ambiguity requests such as returns, billing disputes, and account changes. It has tools for order lookup, refund issuing, and ticket escalation, and it reads from a policy knowledge base. Conversations can run long, and some cases must reach a human." },
    { id: "codegen", title: "Code Generation with Claude Code", text: "Your team uses Claude Code for generation, refactoring, debugging, and documentation across a large shared repository. Engineers work in different subdirectories with different conventions. You are responsible for configuring it so the whole team gets consistent behaviour, and for deciding what belongs in shared configuration versus each developer's own setup." },
    { id: "research", title: "Multi-Agent Research System", text: "You are building a multi-agent research system on the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent receives a research question, decomposes it, and delegates to subagents that search and read sources. Subagents return findings the coordinator synthesises into a report. Runs are long, sources are numerous, and the report must be traceable back to what was actually found." },
    { id: "devtools", title: "Developer Productivity Tooling", text: "You are building internal tooling on the Claude Agent SDK to help engineers explore unfamiliar codebases, understand legacy systems, and answer questions about internal services. It connects to several internal systems through MCP servers, and different teams need access to different subsets of those systems." },
    { id: "cicd", title: "Claude Code in a CI/CD Pipeline", text: "You are integrating Claude Code into a CI/CD pipeline that runs automated code review on pull requests, generates release notes, and triages failing builds. It runs unattended with no interactive terminal, its output is consumed by other pipeline steps, and a failure must not silently block the team's merges." },
    { id: "extract", title: "Structured Data Extraction", text: "You are building a system that extracts structured information from unstructured documents — invoices, contracts, and scanned forms — validates it against a schema, and writes it to a downstream database. Documents vary in quality and completeness, and incorrect data reaching the database is expensive to unwind." },
  ],
  questions: [
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The agent sometimes keeps working after the user's problem is resolved. What should determine when its loop ends?",o:["A phrase in the response text indicating the work is finished","A timer that ends the loop after a set number of seconds","A fixed cap on how many iterations the loop may run for","The stop reason returned with the model's response"],c:[3],e:"Loop termination should be driven by the structured stop reason. Parsing natural language for completion signals is brittle, and caps and timers are safety nets rather than the primary mechanism.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A refund tool must never run before an eligibility check. What is the reliable way to enforce that order?",o:["A tool description on the refund tool noting the check comes first","An example in the prompt showing the correct order being followed","A programmatic hook that blocks the refund call until the check has run","A system prompt instruction stating the required order of the two tools"],c:[2],e:"Financial ordering constraints need programmatic enforcement. Prompt instructions, descriptions, and examples all have a non-zero failure rate that a business rule cannot tolerate.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Which condition is a sound trigger for escalating a support case to a human?",o:["The customer's messages indicate they are becoming frustrated","The conversation has exceeded a set number of exchanged turns","The agent reports low confidence in the answer it has produced","The policy knowledge base does not cover the situation presented"],c:[3],e:"A genuine policy gap is a reliable, checkable trigger. Detected sentiment and self-reported confidence are unreliable signals, and turn count doesn't track difficulty.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Long conversations are summarised to save context, and order numbers are being lost. What is the fix?",o:["Summarise less aggressively so more of the original text survives","Hold transactional details in a facts block that is never summarised","Ask the model to preserve any numbers it encounters when summarising","Move to a model with a larger context window so summarising is rarer"],c:[1],e:"Progressive summarisation paraphrases exact values away. A verbatim facts block carried in every prompt is what protects transactional specifics.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The agent frequently calls the ticket escalation tool when it should call order lookup. What should be fixed first?",o:["The two tool descriptions, so their boundaries are unambiguous","The system prompt, by adding rules about which tool to prefer","The tool set, by merging the two tools into a single broader one","The model tier, by moving to one better at selecting between tools"],c:[0],e:"Misrouting between similar tools is a description problem first. Clarifying what each does and where the boundary lies precedes prompt rules or restructuring.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"What should a strong description for the refund tool specify?",o:["A warning that refunds are a sensitive financial operation","What it does, its expected inputs, and when not to use it","The internal service it calls and the team that maintains it","The average time the underlying refund operation takes to run"],c:[1],e:"Purpose, inputs, and explicit boundaries against similar tools are what drive correct selection. Implementation detail and generic warnings don't help the model choose.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The agent has fourteen tools, several with overlapping purposes. Which consequence follows most directly from the overlap?",o:["It refuses to act until the ambiguity between the tools is resolved","It always defaults to whichever tool appears first in the definitions","It calls every one of the available tools before deciding which result to use","It picks inconsistently between tools whose descriptions do not separate them"],c:[3],e:"Overlapping descriptions leave no basis for choosing, which shows up as inconsistent routing rather than refusal or a fixed fallback.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An order lookup fails and the customer's question cannot be fully answered. What should the agent's reply do?",o:["Hand the conversation to a human without attempting a partial answer","Keep retrying the lookup in the background before replying at all","Give its best estimate of the order details rather than admitting a gap","Answer what it can and state plainly which part it could not retrieve"],c:[3],e:"Partial service with the gap named is more useful than silence, a premature handoff, or an invented figure the customer may act on.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Which part of the agent should own the decision to escalate?",o:["The agent loop, using defined criteria checked on each turn","The refund tool, which can escalate when a case looks unusual","The customer, who can request a human at any point in the conversation","The knowledge base, which flags topics that require human handling"],c:[0],e:"Escalation is a control-flow decision belonging to the loop against explicit criteria. A customer request is one such criterion but not the whole mechanism.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The agent gives inconsistent answers to the same policy question across conversations. What is the most likely cause?",o:["The model is inherently inconsistent on questions of this kind","The policy knowledge base contains too few documents overall","The relevant policy is not being reliably retrieved into context","The conversations are too short for the policy to take effect"],c:[2],e:"Inconsistency on a documented policy usually means retrieval isn't reliably surfacing the governing passage, so the model is answering from general knowledge instead.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A new tool is needed to check warranty status against a third-party system. What should be evaluated first?",o:["Which model tier will call the new tool most reliably","Whether an existing MCP server already exposes that system","How the new tool's description ought to be worded for the model","Which transport the new custom server ought to use"],c:[1],e:"Checking for an existing server before building one avoids duplicating an integration that may already be maintained elsewhere.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The agent occasionally issues a refund larger than the original order. What control addresses this most directly?",o:["Validation inside the refund tool that rejects amounts above the order total","A note in the tool's description about the maximum refund permitted","A review of refunds above the order total after they have been issued","A prompt instruction never to refund more than the original order was worth"],c:[0],e:"A hard constraint belongs in the tool's own validation, where it cannot be talked around. Prompt-level rules and after-the-fact review both allow the bad call through.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Key case details appear in the middle of a very long prompt and are being missed. What is the practical fix?",o:["Move the critical details to the start of the prompt where they hold attention","Ask the model to read the whole of the prompt carefully before answering","Shorten every other part of the prompt so that the details stand out more","Repeat the case details several times throughout the whole body of the prompt"],c:[0],e:"Content buried mid-prompt is the weakest position. Relocating the material that matters to the front is the direct remedy for the lost-in-the-middle effect.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Which tool_choice setting forces the agent to call some tool but lets it pick which?",o:["any","none","a specific named tool","auto"],c:[0],e:"'any' requires a tool call while leaving the selection to the model; 'auto' permits a text-only reply, and naming a tool removes the choice entirely.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The team wants to add a second agent that reviews the first agent's proposed refunds. What makes this effective?",o:["The reviewer uses a faster model tier so the added step stays cheap","The reviewer runs with its own context and a distinct reviewing brief","The reviewer is the same agent asked to re-examine its own decision","The reviewer sees only the refund amount without the surrounding case"],c:[1],e:"Independent review needs separate context and a genuinely different brief. Self-review inherits the reasoning that produced the decision in the first place.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"What is the risk of letting the agent's conversation history grow without management?",o:["The conversation becomes unrecoverable if the session is interrupted","Tool definitions are dropped once the history passes a certain size","The agent begins repeating its earlier responses back to the customer","Early instructions lose influence as later content accumulates"],c:[3],e:"Unmanaged growth dilutes the instructions that were set at the outset, which is why long-running agents need active context management.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The refund tool and a separate 'goodwill credit' tool have nearly identical descriptions. What follows?",o:["The agent will always choose whichever tool is defined first","The API will reject the request for containing ambiguous tools","The agent will route between them unpredictably","The agent will call both tools and let the application decide"],c:[2],e:"Near-identical descriptions leave the model without a basis for choosing, which shows up as unpredictable routing rather than a defined fallback or an error.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R1",t:"m",q:"Which two belong in the agent loop rather than in the prompt? (Select 2)",o:["Checking the stop reason to decide whether to continue","Explaining what the company's returns window is","Describing the tone the agent should use with customers","Enforcing that eligibility is verified before a refund runs"],c:[0,3],e:"Control flow and hard business constraints belong in code; tone and policy content are prompt and knowledge concerns.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A case is escalated to a human. What should be passed along with it?",o:["The agent's confidence score for the answer it could not give","What was attempted, what was established, and why the case was escalated","The full untrimmed transcript of the entire agent conversation","A one-line summary of the customer's original opening message to the agent"],c:[1],e:"A structured handover of attempts, findings, and the escalation reason is what makes the human's job possible without re-reading everything.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The order lookup tool currently returns a paragraph of prose. What is the main argument for returning defined fields instead?",o:["Prose cannot be returned by a tool under the Messages API","Defined fields are routed through a faster processing path","Prose results are discarded from the conversation after one turn","A downstream check can verify a field, but not a sentence"],c:[3],e:"Machine-checkable fields are what let the refund path validate a value before acting on it; prose can only be re-interpreted.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The team building the support agent wants Claude Code restricted to reading and testing, never editing, during investigation work. What enforces that?",o:["A review of the diff before any of the changes are committed","An allowed-tools configuration listing only the read and test tools","A reminder in each prompt that this session is investigation only","A line in the project instructions asking it not to edit files"],c:[1],e:"Tool permissions are enforced by configuration; instructions and after-the-fact review do not prevent an edit from being made.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants to resume yesterday's Claude Code session on the refund tool with its context intact. What supports this?",o:["Re-reading the project configuration at the start of the day","Asking the model to recall what was discussed previously","Pasting yesterday's transcript into a fresh session","Resuming the named session rather than starting a new one"],c:[3],e:"Named session resumption restores the prior context directly. Re-pasting a transcript is lossy and the model cannot recall a session it no longer holds.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A tool used by the support agent returns amounts in cents, but the team wants them normalised to pounds before the model sees them. Where does that belong?",o:["In the system prompt, instructing the model to divide by one hundred","In the model's own reasoning, which can handle the conversion inline","In a post-tool hook that transforms the result before it reaches the model","In the tool's description, explaining that the amounts arrive in cents"],c:[2],e:"Deterministic normalisation belongs in a hook where it always happens, rather than depending on the model applying a conversion correctly every time.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The team wants the agent's policy document pulled into its Claude Code context without pasting it each time. What mechanism fits?",o:["An instruction telling the model to open the file when relevant","An import reference in the project configuration pointing at the file","A slash command that prints the whole document into the session","A copy of the document committed into the project's root directory"],c:[1],e:"An import pulls the file's content into context as configuration is loaded, rather than depending on a manual step or the model choosing to open it.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The agent's replies are accurate but frequently miss the customer's actual question. What should be measured to confirm this?",o:["Whether the reply is shorter than the average reply length","Whether the reply cites the policy document it drew on","Whether the reply addresses the request the ticket actually made","Whether the reply is factually correct on the points it does make"],c:[2],e:"Relevance to the question asked is a separate property from factual accuracy, and it is the one failing here.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"An extraction of the customer's order number fails schema validation. What should the agent do before giving up?",o:["Retry once with the validation error fed back as part of the request","Escalate immediately, since a validation failure indicates a real fault","Return the malformed value and let the downstream step handle it","Retry the identical request, since the failure may have been transient"],c:[0],e:"Feeding the validation error back gives the model the information it needs to correct itself, which an identical retry does not.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A sample of agent conversations is being reviewed for quality. How should the sample be selected?",o:["The most recent conversations, since they reflect current behaviour","Stratified across case types, so each is represented in the review","Conversations the agent itself flagged as difficult to handle","The longest conversations, since they contain the most to assess"],c:[1],e:"Stratified sampling ensures rare but important case types are examined; recency, length, and self-flagging all skew towards a subset.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A tool call returns an error flagged as retryable. What should the loop do?",o:["Ignore the flag, since the agent cannot act on error categories","Treat it the same as any other error and stop the conversation","Retry with backoff, then surface the failure if it persists","Retry immediately and indefinitely until the call succeeds"],c:[2],e:"A retryable classification is actionable: retry with backoff, but bounded, so a persistent failure still surfaces rather than looping forever.",sc:"support"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Team-wide coding standards keep failing to reach new joiners. Where should they live?",o:["In project-level configuration committed to the repository","In each developer's user-level configuration on their own machine","In the prompt each developer writes at the start of a session","In a wiki page that new joiners are asked to read during onboarding"],c:[0],e:"User-level config stays on one machine and never reaches anyone else. Project-level configuration travels with the repository, which is what makes standards shared.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Test conventions need to apply across many directories that don't share a parent. What fits best?",o:["Path-specific rules matched by glob patterns across the codebase","A user-level file maintained by whoever owns the test suite","A single root-level file listing every directory the rules apply to","A directory-level configuration file placed in each affected folder"],c:[0],e:"Glob-matched rules apply across a codebase regardless of location, whereas directory-level files are bound to where they sit.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An engineer is starting a multi-file migration with an unclear approach. What should they use?",o:["A single very detailed prompt describing every edit in advance","A separate session for each file so the changes stay independent","Direct execution, since the migration will need many edits anyway","Plan mode, to explore the approach before any changes are committed"],c:[3],e:"Plan mode suits work where the right approach needs establishing first; direct execution fits clear, contained tasks.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"What belongs in project configuration rather than in a Skill?",o:["Instructions for a workflow used by only one team member","Standards that apply to all work in the repository","A step-by-step guide for generating a particular report","The procedure for a specific recurring task like a release"],c:[1],e:"Universal standards belong in configuration; specific repeatable procedures are what Skills package.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A code review prompt says 'be conservative about flagging issues' and results are inconsistent. What is the fix?",o:["Ask for a confidence score attached to each issue that is raised","Instruct it to flag fewer issues than it did on the previous run","Replace it with categorical criteria stating exactly what to flag","Move to a more capable model tier to improve its judgement"],c:[2],e:"Vague confidence language isn't actionable. Explicit categorical criteria — what counts as an issue — is what produces consistency.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Generated documentation is inconsistent in structure despite detailed written instructions. What usually helps more?",o:["A rule forbidding any deviation from the described structure","A request to re-read the instructions before generating","Two or three concrete examples showing the intended output","A longer and more precise set of written instructions"],c:[2],e:"Concrete examples anchor format more reliably than adding further prose instruction, which is a well-established pattern in prompt design.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A Skill produces very verbose intermediate output that clutters the main session. What addresses this?",o:["Running the Skill in a separate terminal session entirely","Asking the Skill to produce less intermediate output","Truncating the Skill's output before it reaches the session","Running the Skill in an isolated sub-agent context"],c:[3],e:"Context isolation keeps a verbose Skill's working output out of the main conversation while still returning its result.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An engineer's session has drifted and Claude is applying conventions from an unrelated part of the repo. What is the cause?",o:["The model has been switched mid-session without the engineer noticing","Accumulated context from earlier work is still influencing the session","The engineer's user-level configuration has overridden the project's","The repository's configuration files conflict with one another"],c:[1],e:"Carried-over context from earlier tasks is the usual cause of convention bleed, which is why a fresh session helps.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Which tool should be used to find files whose contents match a pattern?",o:["A general-purpose file reading tool","A filename matching tool such as glob","A content search tool such as grep","A directory listing of the repository root"],c:[2],e:"Content search and filename matching are distinct; using the filename tool to search contents is a common and avoidable mistake.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Two configuration files apply to the same file being edited. How should this be designed?",o:["With only the most specific configuration applying and the other ignored","With whichever configuration was modified most recently taking effect","With the two merged into a single file to avoid the overlap entirely","With the more specific configuration layering on top of the broader one"],c:[3],e:"Cascading configuration is the intended model: broader guidance applies, with more specific guidance refining it closer to the code.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Generated code passes review but repeatedly misses an internal library the team prefers. What should change?",o:["Each engineer should mention the library in their own individual prompts","The project configuration should name the preferred library and when to use it","The library should be renamed so that it is easier for the model to find","Review should be tightened so that the omission gets caught before any merge"],c:[1],e:"A durable team preference belongs in shared configuration rather than depending on each engineer remembering to say it.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A long refactoring session is losing track of decisions made an hour earlier. What is the practical remedy?",o:["Continue and re-explain each decision whenever it becomes relevant","Ask the model to recall the decisions it made earlier in the session","Break the refactor into files handled in completely separate sessions","Capture the decisions in a persistent form the session can re-read"],c:[3],e:"Persisting decisions where they can be re-read keeps them authoritative, rather than relying on them surviving in a long conversation.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The team wants Claude Code to reach an internal service. What is the appropriate mechanism?",o:["An MCP server exposing that service's operations as tools","A Skill containing instructions for calling the service by hand","Configuration listing the service's endpoints for reference","A prompt describing the service's API in sufficient detail"],c:[0],e:"MCP is the standard integration path for reaching an external system; Skills and prompts describe procedure rather than providing access.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team judge whether a change to their shared configuration helped?",o:["By running a fixed set of representative tasks before and after","By asking engineers whether the change felt like an improvement","By checking whether the configuration file is shorter than before","By counting how many review comments were raised the following week"],c:[0],e:"A controlled before-and-after on representative tasks is what makes the comparison meaningful; perception and incidental counts are confounded.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A release procedure used only by the release manager is added to project-wide configuration. What is the cost?",o:["The configuration file exceeds the maximum length that is permitted","Every session carries it, including the many that will never run a release","Project configuration stops being read once a Skill exists for the task","A procedure of that kind cannot be expressed in configuration at all"],c:[1],e:"Project configuration loads for all work in the repository, so a narrow procedure placed there occupies context in every unrelated session.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants Claude Code to run a multi-step task without confirming each edit. What should they consider?",o:["Whether the permission bounds configured still cover the risk of those edits","Whether the particular model tier that was selected supports autonomous operation","Whether the repository has been fully backed up before the run is started","Whether the task will complete any faster than it would do interactively"],c:[0],e:"Reducing per-step confirmation still operates inside configured permissions, so the question is whether those bounds are appropriate for the work.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should large generated diffs be reviewed in smaller pieces?",o:["The model produces lower-quality code later in a long generation","Version control systems reject any diff above a certain size threshold","Large diffs cannot be displayed correctly by most review tools","Reviewer attention degrades over a long diff, so errors slip through"],c:[3],e:"The constraint is human review capacity. Breaking work into reviewable units is what keeps the check meaningful.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"m",q:"Which two make a tool set easier for Claude Code to use correctly? (Select 2)",o:["Tool names follow the internal service naming convention","Each tool's description states explicitly when not to use it","Every available tool is exposed so nothing is ever unavailable","The set is scoped to the tools the current task actually needs"],c:[1,3],e:"Explicit boundaries and a scoped set both improve selection accuracy; exposing everything degrades it, and naming conventions are neutral.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A generated test suite passes but does not actually exercise the edge cases. What does this indicate?",o:["The request specified tests but not what they needed to cover","Generated tests are inherently unable to cover edge cases","The existing code is untestable in its current structure","The model tier being used is insufficient for test generation work"],c:[0],e:"Coverage is a requirement that has to be stated. Asking for tests without specifying what they must exercise leaves the selection to the model.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Where should a developer's personal preferences, such as their preferred commit message style, be configured?",o:["In a Skill, so that it can be invoked whenever any commit is being made","At user level, where it applies to their own work without affecting others","In the repository README file, where any other developer is able to read it","At project level, so that the entire team benefits from the convention"],c:[1],e:"Personal preference belongs at user level precisely because it should not be imposed on the team through shared configuration.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A refactor spans forty files and the right approach is not yet settled. Which mode fits the first phase?",o:["One session per file, so each change stays small and independent","A single detailed prompt describing every edit before starting","Direct execution, since the scale means changes will be needed anyway","Plan mode, so the approach is explored before any file is changed"],c:[3],e:"Plan mode suits work whose approach must be established first; committing edits before that is settled is what makes large refactors go wrong.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants Claude Code to stop and ask before running any command that touches the network. What achieves this?",o:["A prompt instruction asking it to check before going online","A review of the session transcript once the work is finished","A note in the project configuration describing the team's policy","A permission rule requiring confirmation for that class of command"],c:[3],e:"Confirmation gates are permission configuration; prompt-level requests and after-the-fact review do not stop the command running.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Generated code is correct but ignores an internal library the team standardises on. What is the durable fix?",o:["Recording the standard in project configuration the whole team shares","Rejecting the generated code in review until it uses the library","Asking each engineer to mention the library in their own prompts","Renaming the library so that the model is more likely to notice it"],c:[0],e:"A team standard belongs in shared configuration, where it applies without anyone having to remember it.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An agentic coding run should stop once the test suite passes. What should signal completion?",o:["The test command's exit status, checked by the loop","A fixed number of iterations after which the loop halts","The absence of further edits in the most recent turn","The model stating in its response that the work is finished"],c:[0],e:"A deterministic external signal is what should end the loop; the model's own account of being finished is not a reliable stopping condition.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An MCP tool returns a result marked as an error rather than throwing. What should the agent loop do with it?",o:["Read the error flag and decide, rather than treating it as a valid result","Terminate the run, since any tool error indicates a broken integration","Retry the call automatically until some non-error result comes back","Treat every returned payload as a successful result to reason over"],c:[0],e:"An error-flagged result is still a result; the loop has to inspect the flag rather than pass the payload on as though the call succeeded.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A long coding session keeps re-reading the same large file. What reduces the waste?",o:["Summarising the whole file and discarding the original content","Re-reading it each turn so the content is guaranteed to be current","Splitting the file into smaller ones so each read is cheaper","Reading the relevant section once and keeping it in working context"],c:[3],e:"Repeated full reads of unchanged content are pure overhead; retaining the relevant part avoids paying for it every turn.",sc:"codegen"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A subagent is given a research subtask but produces work unrelated to the wider question. What is the likely cause?",o:["The subagent inherited the coordinator's context and became confused","The subagent used a different model tier from the coordinator","The coordinator did not pass enough context in the subagent's brief","The subagent's tools were too narrowly scoped for the subtask"],c:[2],e:"Subagents don't inherit the coordinator's conversation, so everything they need must be passed explicitly in their prompt.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A report omits a topic area the question required. The coordinator's delegation log shows no subtask was ever created for it. What does this establish?",o:["The fault is in synthesis, which dropped the section when combining","The fault is in the subagents, which failed to cover their material","The fault is in the sources, which did not contain that topic","The fault is in decomposition, since the work was never assigned"],c:[3],e:"The delegation log is the evidence that distinguishes the causes: with no subtask created, nothing downstream could have covered it.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"How should the coordinator launch three independent research subagents efficiently?",o:["Combine the three subtasks into one larger subagent brief","Launch the first and let it spawn the other two in turn","Launch them one at a time, waiting for each to return","Emit the three delegation calls together in one response"],c:[3],e:"Issuing multiple delegation calls in a single response runs them in parallel and avoids serial round-trip latency.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Can two subagents exchange findings directly with one another?",o:["No, unless the coordinator has finished and released control of the run","No — communication routes through the coordinator that dispatched them","Yes, provided they were launched within the same parallel batch","Yes, if they are configured to share a common context window"],c:[1],e:"Hub-and-spoke orchestration routes everything through the coordinator; subagents have no direct channel between them.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A subagent's exploration produced many dead ends. What should be returned to the coordinator?",o:["Only a numeric confidence score for the subtask's conclusion","The complete working context so the coordinator can audit it","The raw text of every source the subagent opened while working","The findings and their sources, not the full exploration trail"],c:[3],e:"Returning the distilled result is the point of isolation; passing back the whole trail reintroduces the noise it was meant to contain.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The report contains a claim no one can trace to a source. What design change prevents this?",o:["Run the synthesis step twice and keep only the claims in both","Ask the coordinator to verify every claim during synthesis","Require subagents to return each finding with its source attached","Instruct the model not to include unsourced claims in the report"],c:[2],e:"Attribution has to be carried from where the finding was made. Asking synthesis to verify after the fact cannot recover a source that was never captured.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"How many tools should each research subagent be given?",o:["All available tools, so it is never blocked mid-task","Exactly one, so its behaviour stays entirely predictable","A small set relevant to its role, typically a handful","As many as the coordinator itself has been given"],c:[2],e:"Scoping each subagent to the few tools its role needs preserves selection reliability; a large set degrades it.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A synthesis agent has been given web search tools. What is the concern?",o:["Two separate agents searching over the same sources duplicates the work","Search results cannot be combined with the findings returned by subagents","Its role is to integrate findings, so search widens its remit unnecessarily","A synthesis agent is not permitted to call any tools of any kind at all"],c:[2],e:"Tools should match role. Giving the synthesiser search capability blurs responsibility and invites it to redo the researchers' work.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A subagent fails partway through its subtask. What should the coordinator receive?",o:["Nothing, so the coordinator retries the subtask from the beginning","The failure type, what was attempted, and any partial findings","The full stack trace from the underlying tool that failed","A generic error so the coordinator can decide how to proceed"],c:[1],e:"Rich failure propagation lets the coordinator decide intelligently; silence forces blind retries and a bare error discards recoverable work.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"When is a multi-agent design the wrong choice for a research question?",o:["When the relevant sources are spread across many different systems","When the question is narrow enough for one agent to answer directly","When the answer needs to be traceable back to its evidence","When the research is expected to take longer than a few minutes"],c:[1],e:"Orchestration overhead is only justified by genuinely separable work; a narrow question doesn't benefit from being split.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the quality of the research system be evaluated?",o:["By how long a complete research run takes from start to finish","Against questions with known good answers, scored on coverage and accuracy","By whether the finished report reads as fluent and well organised throughout","By the number of distinct sources each run manages to consult"],c:[1],e:"Known-answer questions scored on substantive criteria measure quality; source counts, runtime, and fluency are proxies that can all improve while accuracy falls.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Findings from six subagents exceed what fits comfortably in the synthesis prompt. What is the sound approach?",o:["Synthesise in pairs, combining two subagents' findings at a time","Have subagents return structured summaries sized for synthesis","Drop the findings from whichever subagents returned the least","Move to a model with a larger context window and pass everything"],c:[1],e:"Designing the return format to fit the synthesis step is the structural fix; simply enlarging the window defers the same problem.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A subagent's document-store tool returns results without indicating which are most relevant. What should the tool return instead?",o:["Every matching document, leaving the subagent to judge relevance","The store's query syntax, so the subagent can refine the search","Ranked results carrying the score or ordering the store applied","Only the single best match, so no judgement is needed at all"],c:[2],e:"Passing the store's own ranking through preserves information the subagent cannot reconstruct, without forcing it to read everything.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What determines when the coordinator's own loop should stop?",o:["Whether every delegated subtask has returned and synthesis is complete","Whether a fixed maximum number of subagents has been reached","Whether the finished report has reached a target length measured in words","Whether the elapsed time has passed a configured threshold"],c:[0],e:"Completion is defined by the work being done, with caps and timeouts acting as safety nets rather than the primary condition.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The system produces good reports on technical questions and poor ones on policy questions. What should be investigated?",o:["Whether the model tier is adequate for policy subject matter","Whether policy questions are inherently unsuited to the system","Whether decomposition and source selection suit that question type","Whether the report template needs a different structure for policy"],c:[2],e:"A systematic difference by question type points at how those questions are broken down and which sources are consulted.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"m",q:"Which three should be passed explicitly in a subagent's brief? (Select 3)",o:["The format its findings must come back in","The coordinator's full conversation history to date","The specific question the subagent is responsible for answering","Any constraints on which sources it should treat as authoritative"],c:[0,2,3],e:"Subagents need their own question, a defined return format, and any source constraints; passing the coordinator's full history reintroduces the noise isolation removes.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Two subagents return contradictory findings on the same point. What should the coordinator do?",o:["Surface the disagreement and the evidence rather than silently choosing","Discard both of the findings and omit the point from the final report","Accept whichever finding came from the subagent that ran longer","Re-run both subagents until the two findings come into agreement"],c:[0],e:"A genuine evidential conflict is information. Concealing it by arbitrary selection or omission misrepresents the state of the research.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"What is the most useful thing to log from a research run?",o:["The wall-clock duration of each individual subagent's work","The final report only, since intermediate steps are transient","The total number of tokens that were consumed across the whole of the run","Which subtasks were created, what each returned, and from which sources"],c:[3],e:"Logging the decomposition, returns, and sources is what makes a run diagnosable when the report turns out to be wrong.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The coordinator delegates a subtask that is itself highly complex. What is a reasonable design response?",o:["Let that subagent decompose further rather than forcing a flat structure","Split it arbitrarily into equal parts spread across several subagents","Handle it within the coordinator directly rather than delegating it out","Increase the subagent's tool allocation in order to compensate for it"],c:[0],e:"Nested decomposition matches structure to the problem; arbitrary splitting cuts across natural boundaries and extra tools don't address complexity.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The coordinator's context fills with raw subagent transcripts before synthesis begins. What is the direct consequence?",o:["Prompt caching is invalidated on each turn of the synthesis step","The coordinator is barred from reading any subagent's working notes","The findings it must weigh compete with working material already distilled","The coordinator becomes unable to interpret the subagents' findings"],c:[2],e:"Synthesis needs the findings in view; undistilled transcripts occupy the same budget without adding anything the summaries lack.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An engineer wants a repeatable command that kicks off a standard research run with preset arguments. What fits?",o:["A saved text file the engineer pastes at the start of each session","A shell alias that launches Claude Code with a long prompt string","A project instruction describing the steps a research run follows","A custom slash command carrying an argument hint for its parameters"],c:[3],e:"Slash commands package a reusable invocation, and an argument hint tells the user what it expects. The alternatives rely on manual steps each time.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A long research session is approaching its context limit but the work is not finished. Which action preserves progress?",o:["Starting a fresh session and re-describing the work completed so far","Reducing the number of subagents so less context is generated","Deleting the earliest messages manually until the session fits again","Compacting the session so earlier turns are condensed rather than lost"],c:[3],e:"Compaction condenses the history while keeping what matters. Restarting discards established findings and manual deletion is indiscriminate.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"A subagent's exploration should run without polluting the main research session. Which configuration achieves this?",o:["Instructing the subagent to summarise before returning anything","Limiting the subagent to a smaller number of available tools","Running the subagent in a second terminal window alongside the first","Defining the agent so its work runs in a forked, isolated context"],c:[3],e:"A forked context keeps the subagent's working turns out of the parent session entirely, which instructions and tool limits do not achieve.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The team wants every research session to start with the same standing guidance across several repositories. Where should it live?",o:["In project-level configuration committed to each repository","In each engineer's user-level configuration on their own machine","In a shared document the team is asked to read before starting","In the opening message of every session, typed by the engineer"],c:[0],e:"Project configuration travels with the repository and reaches everyone; user-level settings stay on one machine and manual steps drift.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A search tool intermittently returns an error the coordinator cannot interpret. What should the tool return instead?",o:["A structured error identifying the category and whether a retry may help","A plain-text message describing in prose what happened to go wrong","The underlying exception text taken from the service that failed","An empty result, so the coordinator simply proceeds without it"],c:[0],e:"A structured error category lets the loop decide between retrying, substituting, or surfacing; prose and raw exceptions leave that judgement to guesswork.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Two search tools have overlapping descriptions and the coordinator picks between them inconsistently. What is the first fix?",o:["Adding an explicit routing rule to the coordinator's system prompt","Removing the less frequently used of the two tools entirely","Merging them into a single tool with a mode parameter to select","Rewriting both descriptions to state where each one should not be used"],c:[3],e:"Explicit boundaries between similar tools resolve misrouting at its source, before restructuring the tool set or adding prompt rules.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The research report's quality varies with how the opening question is phrased. What should be standardised?",o:["The number of subagents the coordinator is permitted to launch","A prompt template capturing the framing that reliably works","The maximum length the finished report is allowed to reach","The model tier used for the coordinator on every research run"],c:[1],e:"If phrasing drives quality, capturing the effective phrasing in a reusable template is what makes results consistent.",sc:"research"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Different teams need access to different internal systems. How should this be handled?",o:["Build one merged server exposing all systems behind a single tool","Grant full access and audit usage after the fact each month","Expose every MCP server and rely on teams to use the right one","Scope each team's tool access to the systems their role requires"],c:[3],e:"Least-privilege scoping is both a security control and a reliability one, since a smaller relevant tool set improves selection accuracy.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A team proposes building a custom MCP server for a widely used internal wiki. What most affects whether that is the right call?",o:["Which of the two MCP transports the new server would be built on","Whether an existing server already covers it, and who would maintain a new one","Whether the wiki's total content would fit inside a context window","How many engineers are expected to query the wiki in a typical week"],c:[1],e:"Build-versus-adopt turns on prior art and the ongoing maintenance burden; transport and volume are downstream implementation details.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An engineer asks a question that spans three connected systems. What makes this work reliably?",o:["The agent queries all three of the systems and combines whatever returns","All three systems are merged behind one tool with a mode parameter","The engineer states which system should be queried in their question","Each system's tools are clearly described so the model routes correctly"],c:[3],e:"Clear, distinct descriptions are what let the model route across systems without the user having to direct it manually.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tool explores a large unfamiliar codebase and runs out of context before finishing. What is the design fix?",o:["Restrict the tool to repositories below a fixed size","Switch to the model with the largest available context window","Explore progressively, loading only what each step requires","Load the entire codebase at the start of every session"],c:[2],e:"Progressive discovery keeps context proportional to the question, whereas monolithic loading exhausts it regardless of window size.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Answers about a legacy service are confidently wrong because its documentation is outdated. What addresses this?",o:["Ground answers in the code itself and flag where docs disagree","Instruct the model to treat all documentation as unreliable","Remove the documentation so only the code is ever consulted","Ask engineers to update the documentation before using the tool"],c:[0],e:"Preferring authoritative sources while surfacing disagreement gives engineers the real picture, rather than hiding the conflict.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A tool that queries a production database is being added. What control matters most?",o:["Ensuring its description warns the model to be careful with it","Logging every query it runs for review by the team afterwards","Restricting it to read operations that cannot alter production data","Limiting the number of times it may be called within a single session"],c:[2],e:"A technical restriction to read-only is enforceable; warnings, logging, and call limits don't prevent a destructive statement executing.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tool should answer from internal systems only, but sometimes answers from general knowledge. What is the fix?",o:["Reduce the model tier so that it relies rather less on its own knowledge","Connect more internal systems so that more questions can be answered","Ask engineers to phrase questions so that only internal data applies","Instruct it to answer only from retrieved results and say when it cannot"],c:[3],e:"Bounding the model to retrieved content with an explicit fallback is the direct control on ungrounded answers.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should retrieved results include where they came from?",o:["Sources are required before results can be cached for reuse","Sources reduce the number of tokens that each result consumes","Engineers can verify the answer against the system it came from","Retrieval fails silently unless each result carries a source"],c:[2],e:"Traceability is what makes an answer checkable, which matters most where the tool is being trusted about unfamiliar systems.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team measure whether the tool is genuinely useful?",o:["How many queries the tool handles across the organisation","Whether engineers reach correct answers faster than without it","How many internal systems the tool has been connected to in total","How positively engineers rate the tool in a survey"],c:[1],e:"Time to a correct answer is the outcome the tool exists to improve; usage, coverage, and sentiment can all rise without it.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An MCP server exposes eighteen tools and routing accuracy has fallen. What is the appropriate response?",o:["Rename the tools so their names are more distinctive","Move to a more capable model that handles larger tool sets","Split it so each agent sees only the tools relevant to its role","Add a routing instruction to the system prompt listing all eighteen"],c:[2],e:"Reducing the number of options each agent faces is the reliable fix; naming and prompt-level routing rules scale poorly.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A long exploration session has accumulated many file contents no longer relevant. What helps?",o:["Clearing superseded results from context as the task moves on","Summarising all of the file contents down into a single block","Starting a new session whenever the context becomes large","Asking the model to ignore the files it no longer needs"],c:[0],e:"Actively removing superseded content is more precise than summarising everything or discarding the whole session.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Different teams need different architectural conventions applied to their own repositories. How should this be structured?",o:["One central configuration file lists the conventions for every team","Each engineer configures the conventions for the teams they work with","Each repository carries its own configuration alongside the code it governs","The MCP server's tool descriptions carry each team's conventions"],c:[2],e:"Configuration living with the code keeps conventions correct as repositories move between teams, which a central list or per-engineer setup cannot.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"An engineer asks the read-only tooling to apply a fix rather than explain one. What is the appropriate behaviour?",o:["Explain the change and stop, since write capability was never granted","Escalate the request to a senior engineer for their approval","Refuse the request without explaining what the change would be","Apply the change and then report on what was modified afterwards"],c:[0],e:"The tool should do everything within its granted scope — which includes describing the fix — and simply not act beyond it.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Answers about one particular system are consistently poor. What should be examined?",o:["Whether retrieval over that system is returning the right material","Whether the model tier needs raising for that system only","Whether that system is inherently too complex to answer about","Whether engineers happen to ask worse questions about that system"],c:[0],e:"A system-specific failure points at that system's integration and retrieval quality rather than at the model or the users.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"m",q:"Which two make a tool's description effective for a model choosing between similar tools? (Select 2)",o:["The internal team that maintains the underlying service","An explicit statement of when to use something else instead","The kinds of question it is designed to answer","The programming language the server was implemented in"],c:[1,2],e:"Purpose and an explicit boundary against neighbouring tools are what the model actually selects on; ownership and implementation are invisible to it.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An engineer asks a question whose answer depends on which environment they mean. What should the tool do?",o:["Ask which environment before answering","Answer for production, since that is the most commonly meant","Answer for every environment and let the engineer select","Answer generally without reference to any specific environment"],c:[0],e:"A material ambiguity should be resolved rather than guessed, since a confidently wrong environment-specific answer is worse than a question.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What is the risk of the tool caching answers about internal systems?",o:["Caching prevents the tool from citing its sources correctly","Systems change, so a cached answer can become quietly wrong","Cached answers consume more context than fresh retrieval","Cached answers cannot be attributed to a specific engineer"],c:[1],e:"Staleness is the real hazard when caching answers about systems that evolve independently of the cache.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team decide which internal system to integrate next?",o:["By which team has asked most recently for an integration","By which systems have the best existing documentation","By which systems are easiest to build a connector for","By which systems engineers most often need answers about"],c:[3],e:"Demand for answers is the value signal; ease of integration and documentation quality affect cost, not benefit.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Progressive discovery costs more tool calls than loading a system's context upfront. Why is it still preferred?",o:["Only material the question needs competes for the model's attention","Loading a large system upfront is not technically possible at all","It completes the task in fewer total tool calls than loading upfront","Loading upfront prevents the model from citing where answers came from"],c:[0],e:"The stem concedes the extra calls, so the answer has to be the context benefit: relevance density rather than call efficiency.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A team wants their MCP server reachable by tooling on several machines. Which transport applies?",o:["stdio, which runs the server as a local subprocess of the client","Either one, since both transports support remote and local use equally","Streamable HTTP, for a server running as an independent remote process","Neither, since an MCP server must run on the machine that calls it"],c:[2],e:"stdio is for a local subprocess; reaching a server from several machines is what Streamable HTTP is for.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The tooling needs to declare which MCP servers it connects to, in a form the whole team shares. Where does that belong?",o:["Each engineer's personal settings on their own workstation","An MCP configuration file committed with the project","A section of the project's README describing the servers","An environment variable set on the deployment host only"],c:[1],e:"A committed MCP configuration file is what makes server connections consistent and reviewable across the team.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An MCP server needs an API token that must not be committed to the repository. How should the configuration reference it?",o:["In a separate file added to the repository's ignore list","As an encrypted string, with the decryption key stored alongside it","Through an environment variable the configuration expands at load time","As a placeholder value each engineer replaces before running"],c:[2],e:"Environment-variable expansion keeps the secret out of the file entirely, whereas the alternatives either rely on manual steps or still ship the secret.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Engineers want the tooling to expose a reference document that Claude can read on demand rather than loading it every session. What fits?",o:["An MCP resource the server exposes for retrieval when needed","An import that pulls the document into context at session start","A tool that returns the document's full contents on every call","A Skill containing the document's text in its instructions"],c:[0],e:"Resources are MCP's read-on-demand data category, which is exactly the case for reference material that should not occupy context by default.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The tooling should answer questions but never modify the systems it reads from. How is that guaranteed?",o:["By having an engineer confirm each action before it proceeds","By granting only read-capable tools, with no write operations exposed","By instructing the agent in its prompt that it must not change anything","By logging every action so unwanted changes can be reversed"],c:[1],e:"Capability is bounded by which tools exist. Instructions, logs, and confirmations do not remove the ability to write.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Answers about one internal service are frequently wrong while others are fine. What should be measured first?",o:["Overall answer accuracy measured across all of the connected services","How often engineers ask questions about that particular service","Retrieval quality for that service, judged against known-correct answers","The average latency of the queries issued against that one service"],c:[2],e:"A service-specific failure calls for a service-specific measurement; aggregate accuracy is exactly what hides it.",sc:"devtools"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The pipeline job hangs indefinitely when Claude Code runs. What is the most likely cause?",o:["The pipeline lacks the permission needed to read the repository contents","The repository is too large to be analysed within the job timeout","It is running interactively and waiting for input that never arrives","The model tier selected is not available to automated pipelines"],c:[2],e:"Non-interactive execution has to be requested explicitly; without it the process waits for input a pipeline can never supply.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Downstream pipeline steps need to parse the review output. What should be requested?",o:["A defined structured output format the next step can consume","Plain prose, which downstream steps can search with pattern matching","Whatever format the model considers clearest for a human reader","Output written directly into the pull request as review comments"],c:[0],e:"Machine-consumed output needs a defined structure; parsing prose with pattern matching is fragile and breaks on ordinary variation.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The review step should run on every pull request and developers wait on it. Which processing mode applies?",o:["Synchronous calls, since the result is blocking a merge","Batch processing, since it halves the cost of every review","Batch processing, since pull requests arrive in high volume","Whichever mode has the most capacity available at the time"],c:[0],e:"A blocking pre-merge check cannot use asynchronous batch processing; the cost saving is irrelevant if the result arrives too late.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The nightly release-notes job is latency-tolerant and high volume. What suits it?",o:["Synchronous calls, to keep the job's implementation simple","Whichever processing mode the pre-merge review step already makes use of","Streaming, so progress is visible while the job is running","Batch processing, which trades immediacy for substantially lower cost"],c:[3],e:"An overnight, non-blocking workload is exactly the batch case, where the latency tradeoff costs nothing.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"The review flags many trivial issues and engineers now ignore it. Which change addresses the cause?",o:["Capping the number of findings it may raise per pull request","Moving to a more capable model tier to sharpen its judgement","Stating categorically which findings count as worth raising","Asking it to raise only findings it is highly confident about"],c:[2],e:"Categorical criteria are checkable; a confidence threshold is not, and a cap suppresses real findings alongside trivial ones.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A tool call inside the pipeline fails. What should the job do?",o:["Mark the pull request as approved so that merges are not delayed","Continue on silently so that the pipeline is not blocked by the error","Report the failure clearly and exit in a way the pipeline can act on","Retry indefinitely until the underlying tool becomes available"],c:[2],e:"An unattended job must fail visibly. Silent continuation and auto-approval both let a broken check pass as a working one.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The pipeline needs the same review standards the team uses locally. How is that achieved?",o:["By reading the project configuration committed to the repository","By duplicating the standards into the pipeline's own configuration","By having a senior engineer review the pipeline's output weekly","By passing the standards as a long prompt in the pipeline script"],c:[0],e:"Project configuration in the repository is the single source of truth, so both local and pipeline runs pick up the same standards.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team tell whether the automated review is actually catching real problems?",o:["Ask engineers whether they find the automated review helpful overall","Compare its findings against issues later found in production or review","Count how many issues it raises over the course of a typical week","Check that it raises at least one issue on each and every pull request"],c:[1],e:"Validating against problems that genuinely mattered is the real test; volume and sentiment can both look healthy while the check misses what counts.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The build triage step should investigate failures but never change code. How is that enforced?",o:["By running it against a separate copy of the repository instead","By reviewing its output carefully before any of the changes are applied","By granting it only read and analysis tools, with no write capability","By instructing it in the prompt never to modify any of the files"],c:[2],e:"Capability boundaries are enforced by what tools exist, not by instructions asking the model to refrain.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Review quality varies between pull requests of similar size. What should be examined first?",o:["Whether the diff and its surrounding context are being supplied consistently","Whether the pull requests are submitted at different times of the day","Whether the model is simply inherently inconsistent when reviewing code","Whether different engineers on the team write differently structured code"],c:[0],e:"Inconsistent inputs are the usual driver of inconsistent output, so what actually reaches the model is the first thing to check.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"What should the pipeline do when Claude Code's output cannot be parsed?",o:["Fail the step explicitly rather than proceeding on an assumption","Pass the unparsed output to the next step for it to interpret","Retry once with the same input and continue regardless of result","Skip the step and continue the pipeline as though it passed"],c:[0],e:"An unparseable result is a failure and should surface as one; continuing pretends a check ran when it did not.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Generated release notes occasionally invent a change that was not in the diff. What reduces this?",o:["Asking for shorter release notes with fewer individual entries","Adding a disclaimer noting that the release notes are generated automatically","Grounding the notes strictly in the commit and diff content supplied","Generating the notes twice and keeping entries present in both"],c:[2],e:"Constraining generation to the supplied source is the direct control; length limits and duplicate runs don't prevent invention.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The triage step needs to read build logs from an external system. What is the appropriate integration?",o:["Copying logs into the repository so they are available locally","Giving the step shell access to fetch the logs however it can","An MCP server exposing log retrieval as a scoped tool","Pasting the full logs into the prompt on every pipeline run"],c:[2],e:"A scoped MCP tool is the maintainable path; broad shell access over-grants capability and pasting full logs wastes context.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"The pipeline logs each run's prompt and output so bad reviews can be diagnosed. What must accompany that to make it safe?",o:["An assurance that only build metadata rather than content is retained","A daily summary of the logs circulated to the whole engineering team","A copy of the logs stored in the repository next to the code","Redaction, access control, and a defined retention period for the logs"],c:[3],e:"Logs of this kind capture proprietary code and internal detail, so diagnostic value has to be paired with controls on who sees them and for how long.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"m",q:"Which two failure modes should an unattended pipeline step be designed to avoid? (Select 2)",o:["Running without a human reviewer approving each individual run","Producing output in a machine-readable structured format","Passing silently when the check did not actually run","Blocking every merge when a transient dependency is unavailable"],c:[2,3],e:"A false pass and an over-broad block are the two ways an automated gate misleads the team; structured output and unattended operation are requirements, not faults.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Review cost has grown with the team. Scoping review to changed files is proposed. What does that approach miss?",o:["Formatting problems that appear only in the unchanged portions","Consequences in files the diff touches indirectly rather than directly","Issues in files that happened to be changed earlier in the same release","The overall architecture, which no automated review can assess"],c:[1],e:"A diff-only pass is cheap but blind to cross-file effects, which is why a focused local pass is usually paired with a broader integration pass.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Different repositories need different review standards. How should this be structured?",o:["One shared configuration lists the rules for every repository","Each repository carries its own project-level configuration","Each engineer configures standards for the repositories they own","The pipeline branches on repository name to select a prompt"],c:[1],e:"Configuration living with the repository keeps standards local to the code they govern and travels correctly as repositories change.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A very large pull request exceeds what fits in one review request. What is the sound approach?",o:["Review only the first portion that fits within the limit","Review it in coherent chunks and aggregate the findings","Summarise the diff and review the summary instead","Reject the pull request and ask for it to be split up"],c:[1],e:"Chunked review with aggregation preserves coverage; truncating loses it, and reviewing a summary reviews the wrong artefact.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"What should happen if the review step is unavailable when a pull request is opened?",o:["The team decides in advance whether that blocks or merely warns, and encodes it","The pull request stays blocked until the step becomes available once again","The step is retried silently in the background until it eventually succeeds","The pull request simply proceeds, since the step is only advisory anyway"],c:[0],e:"Whether an unavailable check blocks or warns is a deliberate policy decision that should be made and encoded, not defaulted to.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Why is a schema on the review output not sufficient to guarantee its correctness?",o:["A schema constrains structure, not whether the findings are correct","Schemas cannot express the fields that a code review actually needs","Schema validation is not available in non-interactive runs","Schemas are only applied after the output has been generated"],c:[0],e:"Structural validity and substantive correctness are different properties; a well-formed review can still be wrong about the code.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"A pipeline step should analyse failures but never push a commit. What enforces the boundary?",o:["A prompt instruction stating that it must not push any commits","Running the step against a shallow clone of the repository","Restricting the step to tools with no write or push capability","Requiring a human to approve the pipeline run before it starts"],c:[2],e:"Removing the capability is the enforceable control; instructions and pre-approval do not prevent a push if the tool is available.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"The triage step should hand back a machine-readable verdict the pipeline branches on. What should it return?",o:["A prose summary that the next step searches through for keywords","The full model response, for a later step to interpret","An exit code alone, with the detail written to the build log","A structured result with a defined status field the next step reads"],c:[3],e:"Branching logic needs a defined field to test; keyword-matching prose and unstructured responses both break on ordinary variation.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A build-log MCP tool sometimes returns a partial result when the log is still being written. How should this be represented?",o:["As a result flagged incomplete, so the caller can decide whether to wait","As an empty result, so the step falls back to its default behaviour","As a normal successful result containing whatever was available","As an error, on the basis that an incomplete log cannot support a verdict"],c:[0],e:"Signalling incompleteness lets the caller choose; presenting a partial log as complete invites a verdict based on missing evidence.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The pipeline connects to three MCP servers but each job needs only one. What should each job's configuration do?",o:["Expose all three but instruct the job which to prefer","Expose all three, since the job will select the right one","Expose only the server that job requires","Merge the three servers behind a single shared endpoint"],c:[2],e:"Scoping each job to the server it needs improves both selection reliability and the blast radius if the job misbehaves.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"Release-note generation is submitted as a large batch. What must accompany each request so results can be matched afterwards?",o:["A unique identifier supplied with each individual request","A sequence number, since results return in submission order","A timestamp recorded at the moment each request is submitted","The commit hash alone, which is unique to each release note"],c:[0],e:"Batch results are not guaranteed to return in order, so a unique per-request identifier is what makes them matchable.",sc:"cicd"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Extracted invoice data validates cleanly against its schema, yet wrong values still reach the database. What does this show?",o:["The schema was applied only after the data had been written","Validation applies only to documents above a size threshold","Structural validity says nothing about whether a value is correct","Schemas cannot express the field types that an invoice actually requires"],c:[2],e:"A well-formed record with a wrong total passes every structural check, which is why semantic validation has to sit alongside the schema.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A field is marked required in the schema, but some contracts genuinely do not contain it. What risk does this create?",o:["It causes the extraction to fail so that the document is skipped entirely","It causes the schema to be relaxed automatically for that document","It causes the validator to populate the field with an empty string","It raises the chance the model supplies a value the document does not support"],c:[3],e:"A required field with no source value pressures the model towards filling it. Making the field nullable is what lets genuine absence be reported.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Line items extract correctly but the totals do not reconcile. What check catches this?",o:["A second extraction run compared against the first for agreement","A stricter schema that requires the total field to be a numeric value","A confidence score requested from the model for the total field","An arithmetic validation comparing the total against the line items"],c:[3],e:"A semantic consistency check on the relationship between fields is what catches this; type constraints and repeat runs do not.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"An extraction tool defines a JSON schema for its input. What does that alone guarantee?",o:["That the values returned are always drawn from the supplied source document","That totals returned will reconcile with the line items they summarise","That every field will be present and correctly typed on every call","Nothing on its own — conformance is guaranteed only when strict mode is enabled"],c:[3],e:"A declared schema describes the expected shape; guaranteed conformance requires strict tool use to be turned on. The semantic guarantees never follow from a schema at all.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Scanned forms of poor quality produce unreliable extractions. What is the sound design response?",o:["Extract anyway and correct the errors when they are noticed downstream","Route low-quality documents to human review rather than extracting blindly","Use a more capable model tier on every document in order to compensate","Reject the documents outright and ask the sender to resubmit all of them"],c:[1],e:"Detecting the condition and routing it differently is what protects the database, since blind extraction pushes the cost downstream.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Should extraction be built as an agent or a fixed workflow?",o:["An agent, since documents vary in quality and completeness","Either, since the two approaches perform identically here","A workflow, since the steps are known and repeat identically","An agent, so it can decide which fields matter per document"],c:[2],e:"Known, repeatable steps favour a workflow. Input variation is handled by validation and routing rather than by dynamic control flow.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the extraction system be evaluated before it goes live?",o:["Against documents with known correct values across the quality range","By confirming it processes documents faster than manual entry","By reviewing a sample of the outputs for their general plausibility","By checking that every output validates against the schema"],c:[0],e:"Known-correct ground truth across representative quality is what measures accuracy; schema validity and plausibility miss semantic errors.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"A field is sometimes extracted from the wrong section of a long contract. What helps most?",o:["Requesting a confidence score alongside the extracted value","Instructing where in the document the field is expected to appear","Extracting the field twice and comparing the two results","Shortening the contract before it is passed through for extraction"],c:[1],e:"Locational guidance disambiguates similar-looking content; repeat extraction can make the same mistake twice.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"Overnight extraction is submitted as a batch. Which property of that choice most affects the pipeline's design?",o:["Partial results become available to read while the batch is still running","Results arrive in the order the requests were originally submitted","Failed items are retried automatically until they eventually succeed","Results arrive asynchronously, so the pipeline cannot block waiting on them"],c:[3],e:"Asynchronous return is the design constraint: the pipeline has to poll and reconcile rather than treat the call as a blocking request.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should the extracted output record which document each value came from?",o:["So that the same document is never processed more than one time","So documents can be deleted once extraction has completed","So a wrong value in the database can be traced back and corrected","So the model can be prevented from reprocessing old documents"],c:[2],e:"Provenance is what makes downstream errors correctable, which matters when incorrect data is expensive to unwind.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"A field extracts correctly 97% of the time overall. What should be examined before accepting that figure?",o:["Whether 97% is higher than the accuracy of the manual process","Whether a more capable model tier could raise it above 99%","Whether the failures concentrate in particular document types or sources","Whether the figure was measured on a large enough sample of documents"],c:[2],e:"An aggregate rate can hide a segment failing far more often. Breaking accuracy down by type or source is what reveals whether the average is meaningful.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Where should a human review step sit in the extraction pipeline?",o:["After the write, so errors can be corrected in the database","Before extraction, to check that documents are worth processing","After validation and before anything is written to the database","At the end of each day, reviewing everything processed since"],c:[2],e:"Review belongs immediately before the irreversible step, which is the point at which correction becomes expensive.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"A proposal gives the model a tool that writes extracted records straight to the database. What is the concern?",o:["Nothing validates the record between extraction and a costly-to-unwind write","Database writes are too slow to complete inside a tool call","The model cannot reliably construct valid database statements","A tool is not able to write to an external database under any circumstances"],c:[0],e:"The problem is the missing gate rather than the write itself: validation has to sit between extraction and a write that is expensive to reverse.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Documents arrive in several languages. What should the design account for?",o:["That non-English documents must be translated before extraction","That field labels differ, so location-based rules may not transfer","That each language would require a separate model to be selected","That extraction accuracy is identical regardless of language"],c:[1],e:"Language changes how fields are labelled and laid out, which is what breaks rules tuned to one language's documents.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"m",q:"Which two checks catch errors a schema cannot? (Select 2)",o:["Verifying that totals reconcile with their component values","Confirming every field is present and correctly typed","Confirming the output parses as valid JSON","Confirming extracted values actually appear in the source document"],c:[0,3],e:"Reconciliation and source verification are semantic checks; presence, typing, and parseability are exactly what a schema already covers.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"An extraction returns a plausible supplier name that does not appear in the document. What is this?",o:["A schema violation that validation should have rejected","A retrieval failure caused by poor document quality","A fabricated value, which schema validation will not detect","A formatting error in the way that the field was returned"],c:[2],e:"An invented but well-formed value passes validation cleanly, which is why source verification is needed alongside the schema.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Extraction and validation currently run as one combined step. What is the argument for separating them?",o:["Validation cannot be expressed within an extraction prompt","Combined steps are not supported by the Messages API","Separate steps run faster than a single combined one","Failures become attributable to extraction or to validation"],c:[3],e:"Separation makes failure attributable, which is what allows the right component to be fixed.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R4",t:"s",q:"How should the team handle a document type the system has never seen?",o:["Route it for human handling until it has been evaluated","Process it with a more capable model tier as a precaution","Reject it automatically as an unsupported document type","Process it normally, since the schema will catch any problems"],c:[0],e:"Unvalidated document types shouldn't flow into an expensive-to-unwind database on the assumption that validation will catch everything.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R5",t:"s",q:"Why should the system record the confidence-relevant conditions of each extraction, such as scan quality?",o:["It is required before any extracted data may be written to the database at all","It allows the model to improve its own accuracy on future documents","It reduces the number of documents that end up needing human review","It allows later errors to be correlated with the conditions that produced them"],c:[3],e:"Recording conditions makes patterns in the failures visible, which is what lets the routing rules be improved.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"What should the extraction tool's schema do about a field that is genuinely optional?",o:["Omit it from the schema and extract it in a separate step","Mark it nullable so absence can be reported honestly","Mark it required and accept an empty string when it is missing","Mark it required and let validation reject documents lacking it"],c:[1],e:"Nullable fields let the model report genuine absence rather than being pushed into inventing a value to satisfy the schema.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The extraction pipeline runs Claude Code unattended and the next step parses its output. Which invocation is required?",o:["Non-interactive mode, with the output left as plain prose","Non-interactive mode with a JSON output format specified","Interactive mode, with a longer timeout configured for the job","Interactive mode, with the output redirected to a file"],c:[1],e:"An unattended run needs non-interactive execution so it never waits for input, and a structured output format so the next step can consume it.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"The team wants extracted output validated against a fixed shape as it is produced. Which option supports that directly?",o:["Checking the shape in a later pipeline step and failing if wrong","Describing the required fields in the prompt in careful detail","Asking the model to confirm its output matches the expected shape","Supplying a JSON schema the output is required to conform to"],c:[3],e:"A supplied schema constrains the output as it is generated. Describing the shape or checking afterwards both allow malformed output to be produced first.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"Which tool should be used to locate document templates by filename pattern across the repository?",o:["A filename-matching tool such as glob","A shell command listing every file in the tree","A file-reading tool applied to each directory","A content-search tool such as grep"],c:[0],e:"Filename patterns are what glob is for; grep searches contents, and reading or listing everything is far more expensive than a targeted match.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R3",t:"s",q:"An extraction script needs a small, precise change to one function. Which editing approach fits best?",o:["Asking for the change as a description for the engineer to apply","Appending the corrected function and leaving the original in place","Rewriting the whole file so its contents are known to be consistent","A targeted edit to the specific section that needs changing"],c:[3],e:"A targeted edit is the least invasive change that achieves the result; a full rewrite risks disturbing code that was already correct.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Extraction runs overnight against tens of thousands of documents. What bounds when results are available?",o:["The batch's processing window, within which submitted work completes or expires","The number of parallel workers the pipeline is configured with","The size of the context window used for each individual document","The rate limit that applies to synchronous requests during the working day"],c:[0],e:"Asynchronous batch work is bounded by its processing window; requests not completed inside it expire rather than running indefinitely.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R1",t:"s",q:"Some documents in an overnight batch expire without being processed. What is the appropriate handling?",o:["Accept the gap, since expired items are not charged for","Treat the whole batch as having failed and resubmit all of it","Switch the entire workload to synchronous processing instead","Identify the unprocessed items and resubmit them in a later batch"],c:[3],e:"Expired items are identifiable individually, so resubmitting just those recovers the work without reprocessing what already succeeded.",sc:"extract"},
  {d:"R2",t:"s",q:"The database-write tool should refuse any record whose totals do not reconcile. Where does that check belong?",o:["In a report reviewed the following morning by the finance team","In the tool description, noting that totals are expected to reconcile","Inside the tool, so an unreconciled record cannot be written at all","In the prompt, instructing the model to check before it writes"],c:[2],e:"A hard data-integrity rule belongs where it cannot be bypassed; prompt guidance and later review both allow the bad write to happen.",sc:"extract"},
  ]
});
